# OpenVPN Remote-Access Network

Remote VPN clients use a separate network from the primary LAB-LAN.

The OpenVPN client network is:

```text
10.8.0.0/24
```

The OpenVPN server is hosted by pfSense.

The basic traffic flow is:

```text
Remote Client
     │
     │ Internet
     ▼
pfSense
OpenVPN Server
     │
     ▼
10.8.0.0/24
     │
     ▼
Internal Resources
10.0.0.0/24
```

VPN users are therefore not placed directly into the `10.0.0.0/24` broadcast domain.

---

# Why the VPN Uses a Separate Subnet

Using a dedicated VPN subnet makes remote access easier to identify and control.

```text
Remote Users
10.8.0.0/24
      │
      ▼
   pfSense
      │
 Firewall Rules
      │
      ▼
Internal LAB-LAN
10.0.0.0/24
```

This allows firewall rules to specifically target remote VPN clients.

For example, policies can potentially distinguish between:

```text
Local LAB-LAN traffic
```

and:

```text
Remote VPN traffic
```

without treating both sources as the same network.

---

# Internal VPN Access

Authorized OpenVPN clients can access internal lab resources through pfSense.

Examples include:

```text
10.0.0.1   pfSense
10.0.0.2   Cisco SW1
10.0.0.5   Proxmox
10.0.0.6   DC1
10.0.0.7   Wazuh
```

Access remains subject to pfSense firewall and VPN configuration.

---

# Surfshark VPN Addressing Role

The Surfshark OpenVPN connection has a different purpose from the remote-access VPN.

Remote-access OpenVPN allows external systems to enter the lab securely:

```text
Remote User
    │
    ▼
OpenVPN
    │
    ▼
Home Lab
```

The Surfshark OpenVPN client allows selected internal traffic to leave the lab through a VPN provider:

```text
Internal Device
     │
     ▼
pfSense
     │
     ▼
Surfshark VPN
     │
     ▼
Internet
```

These two VPN functions are separate even though both use OpenVPN technology.

