# VLANs and IP Subnets

A VLAN operates at Layer 2.

An IP subnet operates at Layer 3.

For pfSense to route traffic between VLANs, each routed VLAN should have its own unique IP subnet.

Conceptually:

```text
VLAN 10
LAB-LAN
Subnet A
    │
    ▼
 pfSense
    ▲
    │
VLAN 20
IOT
Subnet B
```

Traffic crossing between these networks must pass through pfSense.

This allows firewall rules to control communication between security zones.

---

# Why One Routed VLAN Should Use One Subnet

Consider two security zones:

```text
VLAN 10
Trusted Infrastructure
```

and:

```text
VLAN 20
IoT Devices
```

If both were treated as one Layer 3 network, pfSense would have less ability to enforce routing policies between them.

Using separate subnets allows pfSense to operate as the Layer 3 boundary:

```text
IoT Device
    │
    ▼
VLAN 20
    │
    ▼
pfSense
    │
 Firewall
    │
    ▼
VLAN 10
    │
    ▼
Trusted Resource
```

This provides a foundation for security segmentation.

