# What I Learned

Building and troubleshooting the IP architecture reinforced how closely related network services are in an enterprise environment.

Important lessons included:

- Critical infrastructure benefits from predictable IP addressing
- DHCP reduces administrative overhead for client systems
- The default gateway determines how traffic leaves a local subnet
- Active Directory relies heavily on DNS
- Domain clients should use Active Directory DNS
- DNS forwarding allows internal and external name resolution to coexist
- VPN clients can use a separate routed subnet
- Routed VLANs require unique IP networks
- DNS failures can appear to be broader network failures
- Static infrastructure addresses simplify firewall configuration
- Organized addressing improves security monitoring
- Network dependencies must be understood before troubleshooting service failures

The most important lesson was that **IP addressing, routing, DNS, DHCP, Active Directory, VLANs, and firewall policies are interconnected**.

A configuration issue in one service can affect multiple systems throughout the environment.

